From 92531d80addb49a2b8f3a64e031708479a4d0701 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iamwrm <iamwrm@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 14:56:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/16] fix(windows): stop leaking win32-input-mode Enter
 sequences into pastes

Multi-line pastes into a remote (Linux) pane inserted literal
`[13;28;13;1;0;0;1_` text after every line end. Two defects combined:

1. parse_win32_input_mode_key_record was stricter than the reference
   decoder (InputStateMachineEngine::_GenerateWin32Key): it required all
   six parameters and rejected extras, while the reference treats every
   parameter as optional (repeat count defaults to 1) and ignores extra
   trailing parameters. Some ConPTY hosts emit a seven-field variant for
   synthesized keys; the failed parse fell through to raw-bytes
   passthrough.

2. Inside a bracketed paste that began as plain text, a decoded win32
   key record pushed its raw sequence bytes instead of its paste payload
   unless the paste itself started from win32-encoded records. ConPTY
   hosts that forward pasted text verbatim still re-encode the Enters
   between pasted lines, so the raw sequence landed in the paste.

Local Windows panes masked both defects because their own ConPTY
re-parses the leaked sequences leniently; remote Linux PTYs display them
verbatim. Parse leniently per the reference decoder, always contribute
the decoded key's paste payload inside pastes, and cover the seven-field
variant, omitted-parameter defaults, and both paste framings with tests.
